ow elevated failures. VDD=0.85, TEMP_C=0 shows 0.096% fail rate. Source: get_overall_yield() filtered by VDD=0.85, TEMP_C=0.\n\n- Mid region: Does not show elevated failures at VDD=0.85, TEMP_C=25 (outer region was 0.134% fail rate, which is comparable to baseline).\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nThe data strongly supports a systematic failure pattern driven by the interaction of three dimensions: VDD=0.85, TEMP_C=25, and wafer spatial position (edge and centre regions). The effect is measurable and reproducible:\n\n- At VDD=0.85, TEMP_C=25 overall: 0.234% fail rate (29 parts failed out of ~3688)\n- At VDD=0.75, TEMP_C=25 overall: 0.136% fail rate (17 parts failed out of ~3682)\n- This represents a **1.72x elevation** in measurement failure rate\n\nThe spatial concentration is particularly pronounced:\n- Edge region at VDD=0.85, TEMP_C=25: 0.272% fail rate\n- Edge region at VDD=0.75, TEMP_C=25: 0.192% fail rate (**1.42x elevation**)\n- Centre region at VDD=0.85, TEMP_C=25: 0.296% fail rate\n- Centre region at VDD=0.75, TEMP_C=25: 0.148% fail rate (**2.0x elevation**)\n\nThe failures are NOT observed at TEMP_C=85 or TEMP_C=0, indicating this is specific to room temperature testing. The primary affected tests are core voltage margining (vmin_sram, vmin_core) and memory functionality (sram_bist), suggesting a voltage-related stress mechanism at room temperature that is most pronounced at wafer edges and center.\n\nRECOMMENDED NEXT STEP\n\nInvestigate whether the VDD=0.85 supply at TEMP_C=25 corresponds to a high-stress condition where die IR drop, power delivery, or thermal gradient variations manifest spatially. Specific actions:\n\n1. **Verify test condition delivery**: Confirm that VDD=0.85 at TEMP_C=25 is being applied consistently across all regions. Measure actual voltage at the test site, particularly for edge and centre die locations.\n\n2. **Check for lot-level root cause**: Query whether the flagged lots have specific VDD/TEMP_C test conditions with higher proportions, or if all lots show this pattern. If concentration is lot-specific, inspect wafer fab or ATE handling for that lot group.\n\n3. **Investigate die-level stress mechanisms**: The spatial pattern (edge + centre failure concentration) suggests either thermal non-uniformity, power delivery network (PDN) stress, or mechanical/interface issues affecting voltage delivery preferentially at wafer periphery and centre. Consider electro-thermal simulation or in-situ voltage measurement.\n\n4. **Evaluate test repeatability**: Confirm whether dies fail consistently at VDD=0.85, TEMP_C=25 or if failures are intermittent, which would point to marginal conditions versus hard defects.\n\nThis is an ongoing condition\u2014failures occur at this test condition consistently\u2014and should be resolved before production release at VDD=0.85, or that condition should be revised if not mandated."
